  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 300 PM Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Overall dry weather through the upcoming week, with \nsome continued signals for increased shower chances by Friday and \nespecially over the weekend.\n\nThe early season nor'easter is currently associated with a 998 mb \nsurface low centered near Atlantic City, NJ. This low will slowly \nlift NE near the New England coast through Tuesday, as weak high \npressure dives down from the Northern Plains to the OH Valley. This \nwill result in continued dry NW flow across central NC, so lowered \nforecast dew points from NBM into the lower-to-mid-50s on Monday and \nupper-50s to lower-60s on Tuesday and Wednesday. After one last \ngusty day today, winds will subside as the low weakens and moves \nfurther away. Meanwhile, central NC will be in a persistent \nsubsident regime between ridging aloft shifting east from the \nSouthern Plains to the Southeast US and the departing low/trough. \nThis will bring mostly sunny skies and dry weather across central NC \nthrough at least Thursday. \n\nSeasonable temperatures today and Monday (highs in the upper-70s to \nlower-80s, lows in the 50s) will give way to a significant warming \ntrend through the rest of the workweek. Forecast highs are in the \nmid-to-upper-80s by Wednesday and Thursday, which could get close to \nsome daily records. \n\nThe next mid/upper trough will approach from the west sometime late \nthis week or this weekend, resulting in increasing precipitation \nchances. However, significant timing differences persist between the \nGFS and ECMWF, with the ECMWF much slower with the trough and thus \nkeeping us mainly dry until late Sunday. The GFS, on the other hand, \nbrings showers into the area as early as Friday, though it is a wet \noutlier on Friday compared to its ensembles. So have POPs ramping up \nfrom around 20-30% on Friday to 30-40% on Saturday and 40-50% on \nSunday.\n\nOne last very warm day is expected on Friday with highs in the upper-\n80s to lower-90s and lows in the mid-to-upper-60s. Behind a cold \nfrontal passage on Friday night, temperatures will turn much more \nseasonable again over the weekend, with highs in the 70s.",
